## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge routing with simple feature flag logic using standard nginx capabilities, keeping it beginner-friendly while still requiring practical setup.

## Key Concepts
- nginx upstreams and map for header-driven routing
- TLS termination in docker-compose
- cross-DC networking and service naming

## Code Example
```nginx
http {
  upstream api-old { server api-old:8080; }
  upstream api-new { server api-new:8080; }
  map $http_x_feature_flag_alpha $target { default api-old; 1 api-new; }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    ssl_certificate /certs/server.crt; ssl_certificate_key /certs/server.key;
    location / { proxy_pass http://$target; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test header-driven routing in CI?
- How would you add health checks and a safe rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a policy-driven, per-tenant response shaping mechanism in a docker-dca setup, using a Config object propagated to the service. It demonstrates practical data-safety concerns and integration with Traefik TLS termination.

## Key Concepts

- Docker Config for policy data
- Per-tenant response shaping and JSON filtering
- Payload transformation without altering business logic
- Traefik TLS edge in multi-DC deployments
- Basic end-to-end testing in a docker-dca context

## Code Example

```go
// Go example: filterFields filters payload by allowed keys
func filterFields(in map[string]interface{}, allowed []string) map[string]interface{} {
    out := make(map[string]interface{})
    set := make(map[string]struct{}, len(allowed))
    for _, k := range allowed { set[k] = struct{}{} }
    for k, v := range in {
        if _, ok := set[k]; ok {
            out[k] = v
        }
    }
    return out
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for a tenant with no allowed fields? What would the response look like?
- What are the security implications if the tenant config is mis-synced between DCs, and how would you detect/mitigate it?

This technical challenge evaluates expertise in multi-data center deployment, dynamic certificate lifecycle management, mutual TLS implementation, and tenant-aware routing patterns. Key assessment areas include: Vault integration for secure secrets management, Docker secrets and configs utilization, Nginx TLS configuration with mTLS enforcement, header-based tenant identification and routing, blue-green canary deployment strategies across data centers, automated bootstrap scripting, and comprehensive observability integration.

```nginx
server {
  listen 443 ssl;
  ssl_certificate /secrets/tenant.crt;
  ssl_certificate_key /secrets/tenant.key;
  ssl_client_certificate /secrets/ca.crt;
  ssl_verify_client on;
}
```
